WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.411 --> 00:00:01.410
LORIN PRICE: Hey, Brian.

00:00:01.410 --> 00:00:03.840
I heard we're demoing
BigQuery GIS today.

00:00:03.840 --> 00:00:05.549
What exactly will
we be looking at?

00:00:05.549 --> 00:00:07.590
BRIAN SUK: Well, Lauren,
you're absolutely right.

00:00:07.590 --> 00:00:11.400
So today I will be using public
data from the US Census and New

00:00:11.400 --> 00:00:13.530
York City Taxi and
Limousine Commission

00:00:13.530 --> 00:00:16.170
to help us analyze
data in different ways

00:00:16.170 --> 00:00:19.170
so that users can make
more informed decisions.

00:00:19.170 --> 00:00:22.020
For example, we can look
at population hotspots

00:00:22.020 --> 00:00:24.540
to see where it might make
sense to place stores.

00:00:24.540 --> 00:00:26.030
LORIN PRICE: Sounds very cool.

00:00:26.030 --> 00:00:27.780
And can you talk about
the feature updates

00:00:27.780 --> 00:00:30.600
made to BigQuery to allow
for these new GIS features?

00:00:30.600 --> 00:00:31.830
BRIAN SUK: Yeah, absolutely.

00:00:31.830 --> 00:00:35.010
So the BigQuery GIS team has
partnered with the Google Earth

00:00:35.010 --> 00:00:37.920
Engine team to integrate
geospatial data types

00:00:37.920 --> 00:00:39.780
and functions into BigQuery.

00:00:39.780 --> 00:00:42.090
A lightweight
visualization tool called

00:00:42.090 --> 00:00:44.580
BigQuery Geo Viz has
also been created

00:00:44.580 --> 00:00:46.830
for users who want to
quickly plot and style

00:00:46.830 --> 00:00:49.869
their geospatial query
results on a map for analysis.

00:00:49.869 --> 00:00:51.160
LORIN PRICE: Let's take a look.

00:00:58.980 --> 00:01:00.480
BRIAN SUK: So I'm
a retailer looking

00:01:00.480 --> 00:01:02.650
to open up a new store
within New York City.

00:01:02.650 --> 00:01:06.030
Specifically, I'm looking at
this particular neighborhood

00:01:06.030 --> 00:01:08.050
around the point
that's shown here.

00:01:08.050 --> 00:01:10.214
If I want to gauge how
successful my store is going

00:01:10.214 --> 00:01:12.630
to be, there's certain questions
that I want to ask around

00:01:12.630 --> 00:01:14.394
that point, things
such as are there

00:01:14.394 --> 00:01:16.560
are a lot of people who
happen to live around there?

00:01:16.560 --> 00:01:19.770
And is there a lot of taxi and
foot traffic around that point?

00:01:19.770 --> 00:01:22.110
Now with the standard
dimensional modeling,

00:01:22.110 --> 00:01:25.380
these are questions that are
not necessarily easy to answer.

00:01:25.380 --> 00:01:27.900
However, this is where
geospatial analysis

00:01:27.900 --> 00:01:29.310
is perfectly suited.

00:01:29.310 --> 00:01:32.140
The first query I'm going
to load up is a simple one.

00:01:32.140 --> 00:01:35.340
This one is quite simply asking
the data set how many people

00:01:35.340 --> 00:01:37.650
live within 1,000
meters of that point

00:01:37.650 --> 00:01:40.235
that we were showing earlier.

00:01:40.235 --> 00:01:41.610
Now when I run
this query, you're

00:01:41.610 --> 00:01:43.825
going to see that
according to the US Census,

00:01:43.825 --> 00:01:47.370
it's stating that there's
113,734 people who

00:01:47.370 --> 00:01:48.780
live around that point.

00:01:48.780 --> 00:01:51.450
But what makes it so easy
is this particular section

00:01:51.450 --> 00:01:52.870
within the WHERE clause.

00:01:52.870 --> 00:01:55.140
There's two things I
want to point out there.

00:01:55.140 --> 00:01:58.500
The first one is this
particular data type here.

00:01:58.500 --> 00:02:01.230
With BigQuery Geospatial
Information Services,

00:02:01.230 --> 00:02:03.520
we introduced new
geography data types,

00:02:03.520 --> 00:02:06.570
which makes things such as
polygons and points on a map

00:02:06.570 --> 00:02:09.394
easy to represent
within a query.

00:02:09.394 --> 00:02:11.310
And there are a number
of different functions,

00:02:11.310 --> 00:02:15.220
such as the ST distance within
function we're using here,

00:02:15.220 --> 00:02:17.520
which makes it very
easy to represent

00:02:17.520 --> 00:02:19.060
those kinds of questions.

00:02:19.060 --> 00:02:21.450
So instead of trying to finagle
a lot of different joins

00:02:21.450 --> 00:02:24.190
and a lot of different ways
to query this information,

00:02:24.190 --> 00:02:26.250
we can now simply
just ask it, within

00:02:26.250 --> 00:02:28.770
this particular
geopoint, how many people

00:02:28.770 --> 00:02:30.720
live within 1,000 meters of it.

00:02:30.720 --> 00:02:33.000
So let's take this use
case a little bit further.

00:02:33.000 --> 00:02:35.880
Before, we were analyzing
one specific store

00:02:35.880 --> 00:02:38.040
and we were trying
to do a simple query.

00:02:38.040 --> 00:02:40.510
But what if we want to
look at more stores?

00:02:40.510 --> 00:02:43.290
What I have set up here
are five different stores

00:02:43.290 --> 00:02:45.150
within the city of
New York, and they're

00:02:45.150 --> 00:02:47.760
going to be represented
by coordinates.

00:02:47.760 --> 00:02:51.180
Each one of these I want to link
up with both the census data,

00:02:51.180 --> 00:02:55.590
but I also want to merge it with
the New York City taxicab data.

00:02:55.590 --> 00:02:57.330
Now this is a
public data set that

00:02:57.330 --> 00:03:00.060
is hosted on Google's
BigQuery in partnership

00:03:00.060 --> 00:03:02.820
with the New York City Taxi
and Limousine Commission.

00:03:02.820 --> 00:03:04.620
And part of this
information also

00:03:04.620 --> 00:03:07.350
provides, for certain years,
longitude and latitude

00:03:07.350 --> 00:03:10.510
coordinate information for
where taxes are dropped off.

00:03:10.510 --> 00:03:12.420
And so now what I
can do is merge this

00:03:12.420 --> 00:03:16.140
all together and not only
get population information,

00:03:16.140 --> 00:03:20.190
but I can also figure out how
many people are being dropped

00:03:20.190 --> 00:03:24.300
off within 100 meters
of each of my stores,

00:03:24.300 --> 00:03:26.430
the idea being if
I have people who

00:03:26.430 --> 00:03:28.350
are going to be
frequenting that area

00:03:28.350 --> 00:03:31.350
or being dropped off constantly,
maybe they can see my store,

00:03:31.350 --> 00:03:33.780
make an impulse buy,
or drop in on the way

00:03:33.780 --> 00:03:35.980
there on to where
they're originally going.

00:03:35.980 --> 00:03:39.360
So if I run this
query, now I have

00:03:39.360 --> 00:03:42.390
points on a map, which I can
place for each of my five

00:03:42.390 --> 00:03:45.980
stores, the population counts
within 1,000 meters of it,

00:03:45.980 --> 00:03:48.780
and I can also have a 100-meter
radius count of the taxis that

00:03:48.780 --> 00:03:50.530
are dropped off there.

00:03:50.530 --> 00:03:53.130
So if I map this
out and I tell it

00:03:53.130 --> 00:03:54.570
that these are
the points I want,

00:03:54.570 --> 00:03:56.730
here are my five
different stores.

00:03:56.730 --> 00:04:03.641
I can now add visual cues
to indicate how many people

00:04:03.641 --> 00:04:04.890
are going to live around that.

00:04:04.890 --> 00:04:06.540
So let's put some
data ranges on it.

00:04:12.240 --> 00:04:14.657
And so now I have circles
of different sizes

00:04:14.657 --> 00:04:16.240
around my points,
which are telling me

00:04:16.240 --> 00:04:18.339
how many people live around it.

00:04:18.339 --> 00:04:20.980
And maybe I want to
change the color of each

00:04:20.980 --> 00:04:23.800
of these different
circles to tell me,

00:04:23.800 --> 00:04:27.130
using the taxi count, how many
people are being dropped off

00:04:27.130 --> 00:04:29.050
in a year around
each of these points.

00:04:34.020 --> 00:04:35.680
So let's give these
different colors,

00:04:35.680 --> 00:04:42.230
going from blue to the
lowest all the way up to red

00:04:42.230 --> 00:04:42.845
as the most.

00:04:45.700 --> 00:04:48.190
And interestingly,
what this is telling me

00:04:48.190 --> 00:04:52.000
is that the area with the
lowest population around one

00:04:52.000 --> 00:04:55.960
of my stores happens to have the
highest volume of taxi dropoffs

00:04:55.960 --> 00:04:58.589
within a 100-meter radius.

00:04:58.589 --> 00:05:00.130
And this is information
I can quickly

00:05:00.130 --> 00:05:04.090
determine using visual analysis
and geospatial analysis

00:05:04.090 --> 00:05:05.650
to make better
business decisions,

00:05:05.650 --> 00:05:08.740
and hopefully make future
decisions much more efficiently

00:05:08.740 --> 00:05:10.542
on where to place other stores.

00:05:10.542 --> 00:05:12.250
LORIN PRICE: So there
you have it, folks.

00:05:12.250 --> 00:05:14.920
Brian was easily able
to use geospatial data

00:05:14.920 --> 00:05:18.700
types in BigQuery GIS to query
information about population

00:05:18.700 --> 00:05:21.675
at an exact longitude
and latitude location

00:05:21.675 --> 00:05:22.300
BRIAN SUK: Yep.

00:05:22.300 --> 00:05:24.370
And I was also able to
plot multiple points

00:05:24.370 --> 00:05:26.890
on a map in the
BigQuery Geo Viz tool,

00:05:26.890 --> 00:05:29.650
and layered in population
and New York City taxicab

00:05:29.650 --> 00:05:32.560
data that can now be used to
influence business decisions

00:05:32.560 --> 00:05:34.180
for retail store owners.

00:05:34.180 --> 00:05:35.860
LORIN PRICE: If you have a
cool project you're working on,

00:05:35.860 --> 00:05:36.460
let us know.

00:05:36.460 --> 00:05:37.870
The link for that's
in the description.

00:05:37.870 --> 00:05:40.130
BRIAN SUK: And also, don't
forget to like the video,

00:05:40.130 --> 00:05:42.380
leave a comment, and
subscribe to the channel.

00:05:42.380 --> 00:05:43.755
LORIN PRICE: Thanks
for watching.

00:05:43.755 --> 00:05:46.890
[MUSIC PLAYING]

